\section{Normalization}{

Utilities are identified only up to level and scale. \code{base} selects the
alternative whose utility is subtracted from all others, and \code{scale} fixes
one parameter to identify the scale. The sampler fixes the error variance
of the first utility difference at one and draws the error covariance by
the marginal data augmentation of Imai and van Dyk (2005), which expands
the scale of the latent utilities in every iteration and returns to the
fixed one afterwards, so that the covariance and the coefficients move
freely. Every retained draw is then rescaled to the normalization in
\code{scale}.
}

\section{Individual choice sets}{

Unordered choices may be made from occasion-specific subsets of the
alternatives. In long format, an occasion lists only the rows of its
available alternatives. The latent utilities of unavailable alternatives
are imputed from their conditional distribution without a truncation, so
they do not restrict the choice. Predictions assign probability zero to
unavailable alternatives. Ordered and ranked models require complete
choice sets.
}

\section{Random effects and mixture models}{

An unnamed \code{random_effects} vector is a shorthand for correlated normal
effects, so \code{random_effects = c("price", "time")} is the same as
\code{random_effects = c(price = "cn", time = "cn")}.

A mixture model divides the deciders into \code{classes} latent classes and
allocates every decider to exactly one of them. \code{latent_class_effects}
decides which mixture model this is:
\itemize{
\item A random effect named there follows a class-specific normal
distribution. This is the latent class mixed probit model, reported as
\verb{mu[<effect>,<class>]} and \verb{Omega[<effect>,<effect>,<class>]}.
\item An effect that is named there but has no random effect is a single
coefficient per class. This is the classical latent class model,
reported as \verb{beta[<effect>,<class>]}.
}
}

\section{Number of latent classes}{

\code{class_update} decides how many of the latent classes are used:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fixed"} keeps all \code{classes} classes occupied, so the posterior is the
finite-mixture posterior given that all of them are used.
\item \code{"sparse"} starts from \code{classes} classes, deliberately more than
expected, and empties the superfluous ones through a small symmetric
Dirichlet weight prior (Rousseau and Mengersen 2011;
Frühwirth-Schnatter and Malsiner-Walli 2019).
\item \code{"dirichlet_process"} creates and removes occupied classes with
Neal's (2000) auxiliary-parameter allocation update, up to
\code{max_classes}. Its precision hyperparameter is updated with the
beta-gamma augmentation of Escobar and West (1995).
\item \code{"weight_based"} splits, removes, and merges classes during warmup
and then keeps their number fixed. Every \code{buffer} iterations it removes the
class below \code{epsmin}, splits the class above \code{epsmax}, or merges
the closest pair of class means below \code{deltamin}, attempting at most
one change in that order. A split moves the two means by \code{deltashift} times
the leading within-class standard deviation. Updates stop after warmup, and
retained iterations condition on the dimension selected separately by each
chain.
}
}

\section{Class labels}{

Numbering the latent classes differently describes the same mixture, every
fit with more than one possible class therefore relabels its
retained draws before they are summarized. The representative assignment
of deciders to classes is the draw that is closest to the posterior
co-clustering matrix in the least-squares sense (Dahl 2006). Every draw
is then renumbered to agree with it, using the equivalence classes
representatives assignment of Papastamoulis and Iliopoulos (2010), and
the same permutation is applied to weights, means, covariances, and
allocations. Finally the classes are numbered by decreasing posterior
mean weight.
}

\section{Prior distribution}{

The prior is conjugate where available; the finite-mixture concentration is
updated by a log-scale Metropolis-Hastings step when it has a gamma
hyperprior. \code{prior} is a named list that overrides the following defaults,
where \code{P_f}, \code{P_l}, and \code{P_r} count the fixed effects without latent
classes, the latent class effects, and the random effects, and \code{J} the
alternatives:
\itemize{
\item \code{fixed_mean} [\code{numeric(P_f)}] and \code{fixed_covariance}
[\code{matrix(P_f, P_f)}]: normal prior for the fixed coefficients without
latent classes, default \code{rep(0, P_f)} and \code{10 * diag(P_f)}.
\item \code{latent_class_mean} [\code{numeric(P_l)}] and \code{latent_class_covariance}
[\code{matrix(P_l, P_l)}]: normal prior for the class-specific values of
the latent class effects, the same for every class, default
\code{rep(0, P_l)} and \code{10 * diag(P_l)}.
\item \code{random_mean} [\code{numeric(P_r)}] and \code{random_mean_covariance}
[\code{matrix(P_r, P_r)}]: normal prior for the means of the random
coefficients, default \code{rep(0, P_r)} and \code{10 * diag(P_r)}.
\item \code{random_covariance_df} [\code{integer(1)}] and \code{random_covariance_scale}
[\code{matrix(P_r, P_r)}]: inverse Wishart prior for the covariance
matrices of the random coefficients, default \code{P_r + 2} and \code{diag(P_r)}.
Entries between uncorrelated random effects must be zero. In a mixture,
both priors apply to the block of the random effects with latent classes
in every class and to the block without latent classes once.
\item \code{class_concentration} [\code{numeric(1)} | named \code{numeric(2)}]: fixed
symmetric Dirichlet concentration for finite weights or precision of the
Dirichlet process. A named \code{c(shape = ..., rate = ...)} instead places a
gamma hyperprior on it. Defaults are \code{1} for a fixed finite or weight-based
mixture,
\code{c(shape = 1, rate = 200)} for a sparse finite mixture, and
\code{c(shape = 2, rate = 4)} for a Dirichlet process mixture.
\item \code{error_covariance_df} [\code{integer(1)}] and \code{error_covariance_scale}
[\code{matrix(J - 1, J - 1)}]: inverse Wishart prior for the unrestricted
error covariance of the utility differences, default \code{J + 1} and
\code{diag(J - 1)}. The prior of the identified covariance is that of the
unrestricted covariance divided by its first diagonal element. Not used
for ordered models.
\item \code{threshold_mean} [\code{numeric(J - 2)}] and \code{threshold_covariance}
[\code{matrix(J - 2, J - 2)}]: normal prior for the logarithmic increments
between the ordered thresholds, default \code{rep(0, J - 2)} and
\code{diag(J - 2)}. Only used for ordered models.
}
}

\section{Specifying the model formula}{


The structure of \code{formula} is \code{choice ~ A | B | C}, i.e., a standard
\code{\link[stats]{formula}} object but with three parts on the right-hand
side, separated by \code{|}, where
\itemize{
\item \code{choice} is the name of the discrete response variable,
\item \code{A} are names of \strong{alternative-specific covariates} with
\strong{a coefficient that is constant across alternatives},
\item \code{B} are names of \strong{covariates that are constant across
alternatives},
\item and \code{C} are names of \strong{alternative-specific covariates}
with \strong{alternative-specific coefficients}.
}

The following rules apply:
\enumerate{
\item By default, intercepts (referred to as alternative-specific
constants, ASCs) are added to the model. They can be removed by adding
\code{+ 0} in the second part, e.g., \code{choice ~ A | B + 0 | C}. To not include
any covariates of the second type but to estimate ASCs, add \code{1} in the
second part, e.g., \code{choice ~ A | 1 | C}. The expression
\code{choice ~ A | 0 | C} is interpreted as no covariates of the second type
and no ASCs.
\item To not include covariates of any type, add \code{0} in the respective
part, e.g., \code{choice ~ 0 | B | C}.
\item Some parts of the formula can be omitted when there is no ambiguity.
For example, \code{choice ~ A} is equivalent to \code{choice ~ A | 1 | 0}.
\item Multiple covariates in one part are separated by a \code{+} sign, e.g.,
\code{choice ~ A1 + A2}.
\item Arithmetic transformations of covariates in all three parts of the
right-hand side are possible via the function \code{I()}, e.g.,
\code{choice ~ I(A1^2 + A2 * 2)}. In this case, a random effect can be defined
for the transformed covariate, e.g.,
\code{random_effects = c("I(A1^2 + A2 * 2)" = "cn")}.
\item Ordered choice models have a single utility per choice occasion.
Their covariates must be placed in the first part, e.g.,
\code{choice ~ age + income}. The intercept (named \code{"ASC"}) is included by
default and removed by \code{0} in the second part.
}

}

\section{Specifying random effects}{


Specify random effects as \code{"<covariate>" = "<distribution>"}. Each covariate
must appear explicitly on the right-hand side of \code{formula}; use \code{"ASC"} for
alternative-specific constants.

Available distributions are:
\itemize{
\item \code{"cn"}: correlated normal
\item \code{"n"}: uncorrelated normal
\item \code{"cln"}: positively signed correlated log-normal
\item \code{"ln"}: positively signed uncorrelated log-normal
\item \code{"cln-"}: negatively signed correlated log-normal
\item \code{"ln-"}: negatively signed uncorrelated log-normal
}

}

\section{Specifying latent class effects}{


The covariates in \code{latent_class_effects} have effects that differ between
the latent classes of a mixture model; use \code{"ASC"} for alternative-specific
constants. A random effect named here has a class-specific mean and
covariance, any other effect a class-specific coefficient. Effects that
are not named are the same in every class, and random effects with and
without latent class effects are uncorrelated. A model with more than one
latent class needs at least one latent class effect.

}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("mlogit", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
### Fit a probit model to panel choice data
data("Train", package = "mlogit")
Train$price_A <- Train$price_A / 100 / 2.20371 # price in Euro
Train$price_B <- Train$price_B / 100 / 2.20371
Train$time_A <- Train$time_A / 60 # time in hours
Train$time_B <- Train$time_B / 60
model <- fit(
  choice ~ price + time + change + factor(comfort) | 0,
  data = Train,
  column_decider = "id",
  column_occasion = "choiceid",
  scale = c(price = -1), # other coefficients are willingness-to-pay
  chains = 1
)
summary(model)
interpret(model)
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
### Simulate choice data and compare the estimates with the truth
set.seed(1)
simulated <- fit(
  choice ~ x | y,
  dgp_parameters = list(beta = c(x = 1, y_B = -1)),
  covariates = list(y = rpois(400, lambda = 3)),
  iterations = 2000,
  chains = 1,
  n_deciders = 400
)
head(model.frame(simulated))
summary(simulated)
confint(simulated)
}
